  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nBetween Friday night and Sunday, a series of upper-level shortwave \ntroughs are progged to track northeastward through the Mid-\nMississippi River Valley. The first of which will pass Friday night \ninto Saturday, but only widespread clouds with isolated light \nshowers or sprinkles are anticipated, since forcing will be mainly \nconfined to mid-levels and there will be a deep dry layer below. \nTherefore, conditions will be mainly dry on Saturday, through \nevening Halloween festivities. With the second shortwave trough, low-\nlevel forcing and moisture will be more favorable for showers late \nSaturday night into into Sunday morning, supported by 30 to 60 \npercent probabilities of measurable rainfall in global ensemble \nmodel guidance. These showers will be light, but probabilities of \n0.25\"+ are 30 to 40 percent across southeastern MO/southwestern IL, \nsuggesting showers could be more persistent and relatively \"heavier\" \nin those areas. With considerable cloud cover both Saturday and \nSunday, high temperatures will be limited to the upper 60 to low-70s \nF and with a small diurnal range including low temperatures in the \n50s to around 60 F. These temperatures are still above average for \nthe end of October/beginning of November.\n\nGlobal model guidance continues to be in agreement that a more \npotent upper-level trough will slowly propagate across the central \nCONUS from Monday through mid to late next week. There is \nconsiderable variability across model guidance solutions on the \nstructure and speed of the trough since it will likely be composed \nof multiple shorter wavelength troughs interacting and phasing (or \nnot phasing) with one another. Although these details are uncertain, \nan anomalous (>90th climatological percentile), deep meridional \ncomponent to the flow with anomalous PW around the 97.5 to 99th \nclimatological percentile and slow system motion all provide strong \nsupport for a widespread rainfall event across the region. NBM \nprobabilities of 1\"+ in 48 hours are 40 to 70 percent across the \nCWA, highest in central and northeastern MO. However, there is \nspread on the timing and duration of this rainfall which will impact \namounts. Temperatures are also uncertain with the NBM interquartile \nreaching around 10 F by late next week, but an underlying cooling \ntrend increases confidence that clouds and precipitation, then \neventual CAA with this system will inevitably cool temperatures \ncloser to average.",
